{"title":"What I’ve been reading","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/930192665/0/marginalrevolution~What-Ive-been-reading.html","date":1764308665000,"content":"<p>1. Thomas Meyer-Wieser, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Cairo-Architectural-Guide-Thomas-Meyer-Wieser/dp/386922813X/ref=sr_1_2?crid=VS517POS4PY1&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.fO5PiGlG4wnW_XTmS_jfMLYoATmCPxOstBicrshYhEyVPX2gpRQsf96Yc0slac52sFrBotOc26X4RwFCOhDWfyPVbFec9aSMVERxIKzljqYR9Fb4KMw2_HL3lA4ceZe-LpMlK8CcSR5uEu2eFSKh--ZwL5DfbQa6SaUTpDmjaeS6eV4b-sJyMPMCcyUA3CbvrBo5AIY7t1j2tPUydnkTzGd1OypMr8r1jXZ8ptxDm1c.xJ2I9AfdSbnFnRbkejhBcIP19FpPXOF90V7HsWOictU&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=Architectural+Guide+Cairo&amp;qid=1764238602&amp;sprefix=architectural+guide+cairo%2Caps%2C117&amp;sr=8-2\" target=\"_blank\">Cairo: Architectural Guide</a>.  A picture book, sort of.  Reading a book on the architectural history of a place, while intrinsically interesting, is also usually the best way to learn the non-architectural history of that same place.  Recommended.</p>\r\n<p>2. Mary Hays, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Memoirs-Emma-Courtney-Mary-Hays-ebook/dp/B00AQML1N2/ref=sr_1_1?crid=3P930015F9AIC&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.pwJ1m3br54MpDWkweLe4Cp7Lk_Ubr_b-B9Nl4pcn1Gz5O79tnRPV4qnZDXYviFzJ452LWGoa75HTadgZ4GWhTbfBUxIEbyxyWspLRc-k31A.-uYoWieiCoNHLYxCACf9W4Zt2K7I7gCQR4GMobHOTzA&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=Memoirs+of+Emma+Courtney&amp;qid=1764247804&amp;sprefix=memoirs+of+emma+courtney%2Caps%2C124&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Memoirs of Emma Courtney</a>.  A late 18th English fictional memoir, still underrated and fairly short to boot.  Very interesting on Enlightenment culture, what it meant to grow up in a reading culture, and the power of early feminism.</p>\r\n<p>3. Sylvia Plath, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Unabridged-Journals-Sylvia-Plath/dp/0385720254/ref=sr_1_1?crid=32GNE3UWLJZ4X&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.mOEn6dVU5nxtYiGHFsDfPeXeBZ7AM1yDqs_pi3Z5kgHvjfPOrDyH2PhJ1TuVgNF7f_OmZF0NFwdiFLamjhZC3izw9DrU665QXpQvgfmJ2rf3-fuJFCtkUio0p1Tm4DDmSRU7A9izKFcSxaD8rgtkBn1PPQgHSiXtcR-gb5TmgEtjyw0N_OeEaCVz0H4eVVoQcW0XTxeHNkRm7qKtmC3CTNE3FJXNism387Ff0DJQr7o.rEm2h6o2p4g6kF7AP_YURmblU1aFdy9G1EH7AGiHt7U&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=sylvia+plath+journals+unabridged&amp;qid=1764238478&amp;sprefix=sylvia+plath+jour%2Caps%2C103&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">The Unabridged Journals of Sylvia Plath</a>.  Usually journals bore me after the first fifty pages.  But this lengthy volume is fascinating throughout, and arguably her greatest achievement?  At the very least worth a try.  She maintained an impossibly high level of writing across these years, plus you see (close up) the shifts in how her life was going, electroshock therapy and all.  Recommended.</p>\r\n<p>4. Somerset Maugham, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/UP-at-Villa-Somerset-Maugham/dp/0375724621/ref=sr_1_1?crid=7VD7ZISEZHVA&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.zbqJr06fvOyRc1d8y3cEgZooVISHsVPMyvk6nExGoVQ6bmsbs0EBKTuG0q6LXuyv8kAJlN_uzyE6chYRJ_uqaYHSo77zgLoq1peuxHRFMg96sLU__4qnUbwtPPtuEMPDqdzFzbxOOeb5pOiSC6zbKFMKqJxKTtIzw3y62OdFNLhl8KZfEWbc2Q8_DZvEvYLGyk7lo0UFefUWJKaX0Rwhr48xotB0LWIQZDg_oTPxk9k.q9c5nCxUYErrvSpZJXKFCbUy9BflvJKz6UZkbL1trxY&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=up+at+the+villa+by+somerset+maugham&amp;qid=1764238437&amp;sprefix=somerset+maugham+villa%2Caps%2C99&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Up at the Villa</a>.  Great fun at first, and very short.  It ends up “overinvesting” in plot, but still for me a worthwhile read.  It is best when at its most psychological.</p>\r\n<p>5. Joel J. Miller, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Idea-Machine-Books-Built-Future/dp/1493088939\" target=\"_blank\">The Idea Machine: How Books Built Our World and Shape Out Future</a>.  A paean to reading and its importance, comprised of many historical anecdotes.  I wish each part went into more detail, nonetheless this is an important book about a cultural transmission method that is in some unfortunate ways diminishing in its cultural centrality.</p>\r\n<p>6. Virginia Woolf, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Three-Guineas-Virginia-Woolf/dp/0156901773/ref=sr_1_1?crid=1F3PLTMK0HLZ3&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.E-xZOWgmEVY46ck3dcu9hqvFSuFDuReH8vf5t0Rvxt3__uBk-VOzwSjmjlQeDvgqX-C5Yy4d7J79u6-WlrbS3k3vResNYYeUl7GSstv-WIIGQl_GFrT1piC0QKxm5WfjgfmxJAushTUEg4VimMKTUIOORDTpzQo9lp0lPrV7MI7GI3TYaosymJG23U01vxoJkOFxD9Isa2J1pg_Na1p72Bq6iGRP9a_viA-VItjysMw.8QxImTug5SOJSX8kLu1PaL2FIT_xzhfvWV6-7c2BAAE&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=virginia+woolf+Three+Guineas&amp;qid=1764247569&amp;sprefix=virginia+woolf+three+guineas%2Caps%2C127&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Three Guineas</a>.  Why do so few people talk about this piece?  It is Woolf writing on feminization and the prevention of war.  The argument is dense, and I will give it a reread.  She seems to attributing some of the worst aspects of militarized society to the approbational propensities of educated women?  She also considers — well ahead of her time — how male and female philanthropy are likely to differ.  In any case, there is more here than at first meets the eye.</p>\r\n<p>There is also Keija Wu’s <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Modern-History-Chinas-Art-Market/dp/1032287969/ref=sr_1_1?crid=2QQSAHBPRHWOD&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.YtBsI2eYHnh3--04CsMtGKaHQLpY9THjzGhZyLShognWDFTGJ2KBUjnuuGbU8a4mpXpJIT0jCiQKEfSpDOIz9LQdej2LIEM9T1epRyiQ-Bnkty0v0RdyX3goFQLkIDykEQ4bVnQC8yY-vseKWXH1xwpSkIIRXcH8tR6Lhvc9Odr9o67G3ssmc3HabEQ-hW_oFB2KMULnPQCVF3ZKkb70OKPbrFg32EjMWWfMRIpepj0.wRe20SQBfmFtqRK93fBbJPMEFguEz3L-BnlQRN0SZfY&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=A+Modern+History+of+China%27s+Art+Market&amp;qid=1764247634&amp;sprefix=a+modern+history+of+china%27s+art+market%2Caps%2C110&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">A Modern History of China’s Art Market</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/11/what-ive-been-reading-280.html\">What I’ve been reading</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/930192665/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/930192665/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/930192665/marginalrevolution,\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/pinterest20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/24/930192665/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/x.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/19/930192665/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/email20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/20/930192665/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/rss20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/11/what-ive-been-reading-280.html#comments\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/comments20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/11/what-ive-been-reading-280.html/feed\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/commentsrss20.png\" /></a> \r\n<div><a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/11/what-ive-been-reading-280.html#comments\"><h3>Comments</h3></a><ul><li><a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/11/what-ive-been-reading-280.html#comment-160989566\">In reply to AG.   I make up to $90 an hour working fro